From ddb79610fc0b49b2327fb266aa72bf4a6d921a3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:09:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(deepseek-v4.1): TileLang kernel backend

VLLM_DS41_KERNEL_BACKEND selects the DeepSeek V4.1 kernel family for the
whole model, drafter included: b12x (default) or tilelang. The model config
requires explicit attention, linear and MoE backends to agree with it.

The TileLang family adds:

- vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang: compressed sparse MLA (split,
  decode and extend schedules with identical bits; SWA-only layers compile
  without the indexed stream; cache pages at any row stride, as BLHNC
  interleaves layers), the MXFP4 indexer (query quantization and index-key
  writes with B12X's exact rounding, scores bounded by each row's live
  length, an exact top-k with multi-level merges, candidate publishing and
  reindexing), MXFP8 block-32 and batch-invariant BF16 GEMMs, and routed
  experts (dispatch, grouped FP8 x FP4 GEMMs over tile-major weights,
  TileKernels' fused SwiGLU with the route weight before the down
  projection, combine). Plans hold no buffers; scratch comes from the vLLM
  workspace, reserved before memory profiling.
- Both tensor-parallel layouts: TP3 (24 heads and 768-wide expert rows per
  rank) and TP4 (16 heads, 576-wide rows). The block-scaled GEMMs take any
  K block that is a multiple of 64 (128 for TP3; 192 and 64 for TP4's
  K=576), reading the two scale words a block straddles and passing the MMA
  its offset, so no operand is padded.
- AttentionBackendEnum.TILELANG and DeepseekV41TileLangAttention, which
  override the layer's indexer and attention hooks.
- tilelang linear and MoE backends: TileLangFP8LinearMethod,
  TileLangLinearMethod and TileLangExperts in the MXFP4 oracle.
- vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/kernels.py, which selects each component's
  implementation, and Block32FP8LinearMethod, the checkpoint weight layout
  both families share.

mHC, rotary, the cache writers, the index-weight scale, the WO projection,
the compressor, Engram, the vocabulary head, the DSpark-specific
projections and vision keep the shared B12X kernels under both families.

The layer now keeps the page geometry computed at construction: the engine
lowers cache_config.block_size after allocation, and re-reading it planned
half-size main pages whenever the worker shared the engine's config.

